\description{
A mixing layer draws contacts afresh every day, by group, instead of
keeping ties. Its contacts are given in one of two ways:
\itemize{
\item A contact matrix, for a few groups that meet each other (e.g., age groups
in public spaces): entry \verb{(g, h)} is the expected number of contacts per day
of an agent in group \code{g} with agents in group \code{h}.
\item A contact probability per pair, for many small groups that mix only within
(households, classrooms): each member meets each other member of its group
with probability \code{contact_prob} per day, whatever the size of the group.
\code{contact_prob = 1} makes every group a clique (everyone meets everyone,
every day), with no ties stored.
}
}
\details{
The groups are fixed (one per agent), or drawn afresh at the start of every
run by a grouping: \code{grouping_random()} draws groups of given sizes at
random.

\code{layer_mixing()} creates one to add to any model with \code{\link[=add_layer]{add_layer()}}. Every
agent of a group takes part in the mixing (the contacts are spread over all
of them), and the agents in the model's infectious states transmit (see
\code{\link[=set_infectious_states]{set_infectious_states()}}; e.g., Infected, not Exposed, in \code{\link[=ModelSEIR]{ModelSEIR()}}).

With \code{set_contact_rate()}, the contacts of a layer with a contact matrix are
a model parameter times the matrix. The parameter is read at the end of
every day, so a global event or a calibration that changes it changes the
next day's contacts. With everyone in one group and a 1 x 1 matrix of 1,
every agent meets "Contact rate" others a day, at random (what the former
connected models, e.g. \code{ModelSIRCONN()}, did; see the example).

Give exactly one of \code{contact_matrix} and \code{contact_prob}. The number of
groups is the number of rows of \code{contact_matrix}, or the length of
\code{contact_prob}; with a single \code{contact_prob}, it is \code{max(groups) + 1}, or
the number of groups of a grouping.

\code{grouping_random()} draws the groups in order, each from the agents not yet
in a group; agents left over are in no group. With \code{as_proportion = TRUE}, a
group takes \code{floor(size * n)} of the \code{n} agents still without a group.

As for every layer, an edit made while the model runs (e.g., from a global
event) lasts for that run only, and one made between runs is kept. A move
made with \code{set_group()} during a day takes effect at the end of the day.
A layer with a contact matrix and one with contact probabilities cannot be
swapped during a run. The groups of a grouping exist once the model has run
(\code{get_group()} and \code{members_of()} read the last draw), and moving an agent
between runs is an error: the next run draws them again.

A layer with contact probabilities that is traced (\code{\link[=enable_tracing]{enable_tracing()}},
before the run) also keeps the run's history of who was in which group and
who was absent (in a state the model takes out of the layers, e.g., isolated
in \code{\link[=ModelSEIRQuarantine]{ModelSEIRQuarantine()}}). \code{groupmates_of()} reads it: who shared a group
with \code{agent}, both present, between days \code{from} and \code{to}. With
\code{contact_prob = 1} that is exactly who the agent met; below 1, who it could
have met ("notify the household"). The history covers whole runs: tracing
turned on during a run, or a grouping edited after it, gives none (an
error) until the next run.
}
\examples{
# 1,000 agents in households of 4: everyone meets their housemates daily
sir <- ModelSIR(
  name              = "flu",
  n                 = 1000,
  prevalence        = .01,
  transmission_prob = .2,
  recovery_rate     = .1
)

households <- layer_mixing(
  "households",
  groups       = (0:999) \%/\% 4,
  contact_prob = 1
)
add_layer(sir, households)

# A daily contact matrix between two groups, children and adults
cm <- matrix(c(10, 3, 3, 6), nrow = 2)
add_layer(
  sir,
  layer_mixing(
    "community",
    groups         = rep(0:1, c(200, 800)),
    contact_matrix = cm
  )
)

# Two schools of 300 and 200, drawn at random at the start of every run
schools <- layer_mixing(
  "schools",
  groups       = grouping_random(c(300, 200)),
  contact_prob = .05
)
add_layer(sir, schools)

enable_tracing(households)
run(sir, ndays = 50, seed = 1)
members_of(households, 0)
length(members_of(schools, 1))
get_contact_prob(households)

# Who shared a household with agent 0 in the last week
groupmates_of(households, 0, from = 44, to = 50)

# Random mixing: everyone meets "Contact rate" others a day
seir <- ModelSEIR(
  name              = "flu",
  n                 = 1000,
  prevalence        = .01,
  transmission_prob = .1,
  latent_period     = 3,
  recovery_rate     = 1 / 7
)
add_param(seir, "Contact rate", 4)

everyone <- layer_mixing("everyone", groups = rep(0, 1000), contact_matrix = 1)
set_contact_rate(everyone, "Contact rate")
add_layer(seir, everyone)

run(seir, ndays = 50, seed = 1)
}
